/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ctrl11_esterification_jacket  --  the first ctrl case whose FIRST LAW closes.

  Every other case under tutorials/ctrl/ runs on the fictitious species compA
  and compB.  They carry no standardThermochemistry, deliberately: a made-up
  substance has no elements and no heat of formation, so there is no datum for
  an energy balance to be on.  The ledger said so and refused -- correctly,
  and, for as long as the corpus had nothing else, invisibly.

  This case is a real esterification (AcOH + EtOH -> EtOAc + H2O, the same
  chemistry as cstr06_jacketed) under PID jacket control.  All four species
  carry the elements datum, so the vessel's stored enthalpy H(n,T) exists, the
  temperature ODE is that H's exact derivative, and the run CLAIMS:

      [balance] energy: H(t)-H(0) = ... kJ vs integral(in-out+Q) = ... kJ

  The residual is NOT a test of the physics -- the ODE was derived from that
  identity, so it cannot disagree with it analytically.  It measures what the
  INTEGRATOR did to the identity: RK4 at this step size, the controller firing
  and re-basing the trapezoid, the accepted-state sampling.  Halve deltaT and
  it falls by four, which says which of those dominates:

      deltaT [s]     energy_closure_rel
        1.0            5.70e-07
        0.5            1.42e-07        <- shipped
        0.25           3.56e-08
        0.125          8.90e-09

  Second order, cleanly.  RK4 is fourth; the TRAPEZOID that accumulates the
  boundary fluxes is second, so what this number measures is the quadrature
  on the ledger, not the error in the trajectory.  Worth knowing before
  reading any first-law residual as a physical statement -- here and in the
  batch campaign ledgers, which teach the same lesson in the other time
  regime.

  WHAT THIS CASE DOES NOT PROVE.  All four species declare a CONSTANT liquid
  Cp, and with a constant Cp the exact inlet term INT(Cp dT) and the
  linearisation Cp(T)*(T_in - T) are literally the same number.  So this case
  exercises the ledger identity and the temperature-dependent heat of
  reaction, and says nothing at all about the inlet term.  The gate
  (bin/curate/check_ctrl_balance.py) carries a fixture that gives ethanol's
  Cp a slope for exactly that reason -- with it, the linearisation costs a
  factor of 400 in the closure.

  What to look at:
      * the balance trajectory gains five energy columns that a compA case
        does not have at all -- the columns exist only where the claim does;
      * its metadata sidecar says energy_available,1 with the functional and
        the datum named;
      * ctrl01 beside it still says energy_available,0 and names the species
        that has no datum.  Both are correct; the difference is the data.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoCtrl;
description   "Jacketed esterification CSTR under PID temperature control: the first ctrl case whose stored enthalpy exists, so the first-law ledger claims closure instead of refusing";

verbosity     3;

startTime       0;        // s
endTime         600;      // s
deltaT          0.5;      // s
writeInterval   10;       // s
